<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Telling-and-practice activity: velocity, acceleration, and concavity.
     Arc: the derivative as a rate of change (Section 1), direction of
     motion from sign analysis of v(t) (Section 2), the second derivative
     as acceleration and the increasing or decreasing behavior of v(t)
     (Section 3), then concavity from the sign of the second derivative,
     applied to sketching position from velocity (Section 4).

     Timing: aim for about 30 minutes of material. Section 1 is a quick
     interpretation check. Sections 2 and 3 are the core sign analysis
     practice. Section 4 is mostly the two sketches. If the period runs
     long, trim in this order: Section 2 task 2 (the quadratic formula
     example), the second sketch in Section 4, then Section 1.

     Convention: t is allowed to be any real number (negative t means
     time before the clock was started), so every sign analysis is done
     on all real numbers and critical points of v(t) are interior.

     Answer key
     Section 1: units of s'(t) are meters per second. s'(5) = -3 means
       the cyclist is moving west (toward smaller position values) at
       3 meters per second at t = 5. s'(8) = 0 means the cyclist is
       momentarily at rest at t = 8, possibly turning around.
     Section 2 task 1: s(t) = t^3 - 6t^2 + 9t.
       v(t) = 3t^2 - 12t + 9 = 3(t-1)(t-3), t = 1, 3.
       v positive on (-inf, 1) and (3, inf), negative on (1, 3).
       s increasing on (-inf, 1) and (3, inf), decreasing on (1, 3).
       (s(1) = 4 and s(3) = 0, for reference.)
     Section 2 task 2: s(t) = t^3 - 3t^2 - 6t + 10.
       v(t) = 3t^2 - 6t - 6 = 3(t^2 - 2t - 2), which does not factor
       over the integers. Quadratic formula gives t = 1 +/- sqrt(3),
       about -0.73 and 2.73. v positive on (-inf, 1-sqrt(3)) and
       (1+sqrt(3), inf), negative between. s increasing, decreasing,
       increasing in that order.
     Section 3: v(t) = 4 - t^2 = (2-t)(2+t), zeros at t = -2, 2.
       v negative on (-inf, -2), positive on (-2, 2), negative on
       (2, inf). So s decreasing, then increasing, then decreasing.
       a(t) = v'(t) = -2t, zero at t = 0 (the critical point of v).
       a positive on (-inf, 0), so v increasing there. a negative on
       (0, inf), so v decreasing there. In words: on (-inf, 0) the slope
       of the graph of s is increasing, and on (0, inf) it is
       decreasing. This sets up concavity in Section 4.
     Section 4 sketch 1: v(t) = 4 - t^2, with s(0) = 0 the position is
       s(t) = 4t - t^3/3 (students do not need the formula).
       Local minimum at t = -2 (s = -16/3), local maximum at t = 2
       (s = 16/3). Concave up on (-inf, 0), concave down on (0, inf),
       inflection point at t = 0 with s = 0 and a nonzero (steepest)
       slope v(0) = 4. The graph is symmetric through the origin.
     Section 4 sketch 2: v(t) = 6t - t^3 = t(6 - t^2), zeros at t = 0
       and t = +/- sqrt(6), about +/- 2.45. v positive on (-inf, -sqrt6),
       negative on (-sqrt6, 0), positive on (0, sqrt6), negative on
       (sqrt6, inf). So s is increasing, decreasing, increasing,
       decreasing. a(t) = 6 - 3t^2 = 3(2 - t^2), zeros at t = +/- sqrt(2),
       about +/- 1.41. a negative on (-inf, -sqrt2), positive on
       (-sqrt2, sqrt2), negative on (sqrt2, inf). So s is concave down,
       concave up, concave down.
       With s(0) = 0, s(t) = 3t^2 - t^4/4. Local maxima at t = +/- sqrt6
       with s = 9, local minimum at t = 0 with s = 0, inflection points
       at t = +/- sqrt2 with s = 5. The graph is symmetric about the
       vertical axis and looks like an M. -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-velocity-acceleration">
  <title>Velocity, Acceleration, and Concavity</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      So far we have thought of the derivative <m>f'(x)</m> graphically, as the slope of the tangent line to the graph of <m>f(x)</m>.
      A slope computes the ratio of a change in the output to a change in the input, 
      which means it measures how fast the output is changing with respect to the input.
      In this activity we give the derivative a physical meaning in the setting of motion, where a rate of change is its own measurable quantity (velocity).
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the derivative (velocity) is itself a measurable function of time, 
      we can talk about <em>its</em> derivative.
      That gives us a way to describe how the slope itself is changing, which is exactly what concavity means.
      Throughout this activity, we allow the time <m>t</m> to be any real number, where negative values mean times before the clock was started.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-motion-velocity" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: Rate of change as velocity</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose an object moves along a straight line, such as a road or a number line.
        It could be horizontal like a track or vertical like a ball thrown up into the air.
        Let <m>s(t)</m> measure the object's position at time <m>t</m>.
        The slope of the graph of <m>s(t)</m> gives the rate of change in position with respect to the change in time.
        If position is measured in meters and time in seconds, then the slope has units of meters per second.
        That should be a familiar kind of quantity.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Velocity</title>
        <p>
          If <m>s(t)</m> is the position of an object at time <m>t</m>, then the derivative
          <md>
            <mrow>v(t) = s'(t)</mrow>
          </md>
          is the <term>velocity</term> of the object.
          Graphically, <m>v(t)</m> gives the slope of the graph of <m>s(t)</m> at each value <m>t</m>.
          Physically, <m>v(t)</m> is the rate of change of position, with units of distance per unit of time.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        Velocity has a sign, and the sign tells us the direction of motion.
        If <m>v(t)</m> is positive, then the object is moving toward the positive direction, so <m>s(t)</m> is increasing.
        If <m>v(t)</m> is negative, then the position <m>s(t)</m> is decreasing and the object is moving in the negative direction.
        If <m>v(t)=0</m>, then the object is instantaneously not moving in either direction. This typically corresponds to a critical point of position where it might change direction.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-motion-units" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A cyclist rides along a straight road.
          Let <m>s(t)</m> be the cyclist's distance in meters east of a signpost, <m>t</m> seconds after a stopwatch is started.
        </p>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>What are the units of <m>s'(t)</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Suppose <m>s'(5)=-3</m>. Describe in words what the cyclist is doing at <m>t=5</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Suppose <m>s'(8)=0</m>. What can you say about the cyclist at <m>t=8</m>?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-motion-direction" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: Direction of motion</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        We can use critical points and sign analysis, as we did when we studied graph behavior, to find the intervals of time on which the position is increasing and decreasing.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Direction of Motion from the Velocity</title>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Compute the velocity <m>v(t)=s'(t)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Factor <m>v(t)</m> completely and solve <m>v(t)=0</m>. These are the critical points of <m>s(t)</m>, where the object is momentarily at rest.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use sign analysis of <m>v(t)</m> on the intervals between the critical points.</p></li>
            <li><p>On intervals where <m>v(t)\gt 0</m>, the position <m>s(t)</m> is increasing. On intervals where <m>v(t)\lt 0</m>, the position <m>s(t)</m> is decreasing.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        For each position function below, with <m>s</m> in meters and <m>t</m> in seconds, answer the following.
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>Find the velocity <m>v(t)=s'(t)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Factor <m>v(t)</m> completely and solve <m>v(t)=0</m>. Use the quadratic formula if the quadratic factor does not factor.</p></li>
          <li><p>Use sign analysis to find the intervals where <m>v(t)</m> is positive and where it is negative.</p></li>
          <li><p>State the intervals where <m>s(t)</m> is increasing and where it is decreasing, and describe the direction of motion on each interval in words.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-motion-direction-1" workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>s(t)=t^3-6t^2+9t</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-motion-direction-2" workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>s(t)=t^3-3t^2-6t+10</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-motion-acceleration" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 3: Acceleration and the second derivative</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The velocity <m>v(t)</m> is itself a function of time, so it has a derivative too.
        The rate of change of velocity measures the change in velocity per unit of time.
        With velocity in meters per second and time in seconds, its units are meters per second per second, written m/s<m>^2</m>.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Acceleration and the Second Derivative</title>
        <p>
          The rate of change of velocity is called the <term>acceleration</term> of the object:
          <md>
            <mrow>a(t) = v'(t)</mrow>
          </md>.
          In calculus, the derivative of a derivative is called the <term>second derivative</term>.
          Since <m>v(t)=s'(t)</m>, acceleration is the second derivative of position with respect to time,
          <md>
            <mrow>a(t) = s''(t)</mrow>
          </md>.
          The number of primes tells us how many derivatives have been taken.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        We can apply the same tools to velocity that we applied to position.
        The solutions of <m>v'(t)=0</m> are the critical points of the velocity.
        Sign analysis of <m>a(t)=v'(t)</m> tells us where <m>v(t)</m> is increasing (where <m>a(t)\gt 0</m>) and where <m>v(t)</m> is decreasing (where <m>a(t)\lt 0</m>).
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Note:</em> Velocity has a sign that depends on the direction of motion.
        So <q><m>v(t)</m> is increasing</q> means that the value of <m>v(t)</m> moves to the right on the number line, as when it goes from <m>-5</m> to <m>-1</m> or from <m>-1</m> to <m>3</m>.
        It is not about getting faster or slower.
        For example, a velocity that goes from <m>-1</m> to <m>-5</m> is decreasing, even though the object is moving faster.
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose an object has velocity <m>v(t)=4-t^2</m> meters per second.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-motion-accel-sign" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>Factor <m>v(t)</m> and solve <m>v(t)=0</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>On what intervals is <m>v(t)</m> positive? On what intervals is it negative?</p></li>
          <li><p>What does this tell you about how the position <m>s(t)</m> is changing on each interval?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-motion-accel-trend" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>Find the acceleration <m>a(t)=v'(t)</m>, and solve <m>v'(t)=0</m> to find the critical points of the velocity.</p></li>
          <li><p>Use sign analysis of <m>a(t)</m> to find the intervals where <m>v(t)</m> is increasing and where it is decreasing.</p></li>
          <li><p>What does this tell you about how the position <m>s(t)</m> is changing on each interval? Remember that <m>v(t)</m> is the slope of the graph of <m>s(t)</m>, and describe what is happening to that slope.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-motion-concavity" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 4: Concavity and sketching position from velocity</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        In the last task you described how the slope of the graph of <m>s(t)</m> was changing.
        That is the idea behind concavity, and it applies to any function, not just to position.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Concavity and the Second Derivative</title>
        <p>
          Concavity is defined by how the slope is changing.
          A function <m>f(x)</m> is <term>concave up</term> on an interval if the derivative <m>f'(x)</m> is increasing on that interval.
          A function <m>f(x)</m> is <term>concave down</term> on an interval if the derivative <m>f'(x)</m> is decreasing on that interval.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because <m>f''(x)</m> is what we use to find where <m>f'(x)</m> is increasing or decreasing, sign analysis of <m>f''(x)</m> determines the concavity:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>If <m>f''(x)</m> is positive on an interval, then <m>f(x)</m> is concave up on the interval.</p></li>
          <li><p>If <m>f''(x)</m> is negative on an interval, then <m>f(x)</m> is concave down on the interval.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A point where the concavity changes is an inflection point, so it occurs where <m>f''(x)</m> changes sign.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        For position, <m>f''</m> is the acceleration.
        Together, the sign of the velocity <m>v(t)=s'(t)</m> and the sign of the acceleration <m>a(t)=s''(t)</m> describe the full shape of the graph of <m>s(t)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For each velocity function below, sketch a graph of the position <m>s(t)</m> by following these steps.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Use sign analysis of <m>v(t)</m> to find the intervals where <m>s(t)</m> is increasing and decreasing.</p></li>
        <li><p>Find <m>a(t)=v'(t)</m> and use sign analysis to find the intervals where <m>s(t)</m> is concave up and concave down.</p></li>
        <li><p>Record both results on number lines with the break points marked, as in the charts from the graph behavior activity.</p></li>
        <li><p>Sketch <m>s(t)</m> so that it has all of the behavior on your number lines. Velocity only tells us how position changes, so assume <m>s(0)=0</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Mark and label each local maximum, local minimum, and inflection point on your sketch, and state the <m>t</m>-value where each occurs.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-motion-sketch-1" workspace="3.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>v(t)=4-t^2</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          You already did most of the sign analysis for this velocity in Section 3.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-motion-sketch-2" workspace="3.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>v(t)=6t-t^3</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          Start by pulling out the common factor <m>t</m> in <m>v(t)</m>.
          To solve <m>a(t)=0</m>, isolate <m>t^2</m> and take square roots.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
